# OpenAPI documents

`howdy_openapi` is an optional package that documents a JSON API as an
[OpenAPI 3.1](https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.1.0) document, and serves an
interactive reference for it.

```toml
howdy_openapi = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "openapi" }
```

`howdy_openapi` is not in the v2.0.0 release, so depend on the `v2` branch until the next tag.

Gleam cannot look inside a decoder or a handler, so there is nothing to read
a document from after the fact. Instead you describe each value once, as a
[schema](/docs/openapi/schemas), and that one description is the decoder a
handler reads with, the encoder a response writes with, and what the
document shows. The document cannot drift from what the server really
accepts, because the server uses it.

## Endpoints

An endpoint is a handler that declares its inputs with schemas. Mount it on
an ordinary controller with `endpoint.get`, `endpoint.post`, `endpoint.put`,
`endpoint.patch` or `endpoint.delete`:

```gleam
import howdy/controller
import howdy/openapi/endpoint.{type Endpoint}
import howdy/openapi/schema
import howdy/service

pub fn controller() -> controller.Controller {
  controller.new("user")
  |> endpoint.get("/:id", by_id())
  |> endpoint.post("/", create())
}

fn by_id() -> Endpoint(Nil) {
  use <- endpoint.describe([
    endpoint.summary("Find a user"),
    endpoint.response(200, "The user", user.user()),
    endpoint.error(404, "No user has this id"),
  ])
  use id <- endpoint.path("id", schema.int())
  use ctx <- endpoint.handle
  user_service.find(id)
  |> service.respond(ctx, schema.to_json(_, user.user()))
}

fn create() -> Endpoint(Nil) {
  use <- endpoint.describe([
    endpoint.summary("Create a user"),
    endpoint.response(201, "The new user", user.user()),
  ])
  use input <- endpoint.body(user.new_user())
  use ctx <- endpoint.handle
  user_service.create(input)
  |> service.created(ctx, schema.to_json(_, user.user()))
}
```

The inputs are:

- `endpoint.path(name, schema)`: a `:name` or `*name` path segment.
- `endpoint.query(name, schema)` and `endpoint.optional_query`: a query
  parameter. With `schema.list`, every occurrence of the key, as in
  `?tag=a&tag=b`.
- `endpoint.header(name, schema)` and `endpoint.optional_header`.
- `endpoint.body(schema)`: a JSON body, up to one mebibyte;
  `endpoint.body_with_limit` takes another limit.

Each runs in order before `endpoint.handle`, and stops the request if it
fails:

- A missing or malformed parameter answers `400` with a message such as
  `{"error":"parameter id must be an integer"}`, as `howdy/param` and
  `howdy/query` do.
- A body that cannot be read or is not JSON answers `400`.
- A body that does not match its schema answers `422` with every field
  error, as `howdy/validate` does:
  `{"error":"validation failed","code":"validation","fields":[{"field":"age","code":"too_small","message":"must be at least 13","minimum":13}]}`.
  Nested fields are named by path, such as `address.city` or `tags.0`.

An endpoint is documented by running its inputs with placeholder values,
such as `0` for an integer. So do the work inside `endpoint.handle`, never
between the inputs, and documenting an endpoint will never run it.

Controller guards and middleware work as they do for any route. An endpoint
on a guarded controller is an `Endpoint(guard)`, and its handler sees
`ctx.guard`. Gleam cannot tell the guard's type inside the handler, so name
it when you use `ctx.guard`:

```gleam
fn mine() -> Endpoint(Principal) {
  use <- endpoint.describe([endpoint.security("session")])
  use ctx: GuardedContext(Principal) <- endpoint.handle
  controller.text(ctx, ctx.guard.user.email)
}
```

`endpoint.wrap` runs middleware around one endpoint.

## Describing an endpoint

`endpoint.describe` takes a list of docs:

- `summary` and `description`.
- `response(status, description, schema)` for a JSON response. Encode the
  response with the same schema, through `schema.to_json`.
- `empty_response(status, description)` for one without a body, such as
  `204`.
- `error(status, description)` for Howdy's error body, the one
  `service.error_response` sends.
- `tag(name)`, to group endpoints. Without one, an endpoint is tagged with
  the first fixed segment of its path, such as `user`.
- `operation_id(id)`, which client generators use as a function name.
  Without one it is made from the method and path: `get_user_by_id` for
  `GET /user/:id`.
- `security(scheme)`, naming a scheme declared on the document.
- `deprecated()`.

Endpoints with inputs document their `400` response, and endpoints with a
body their `422`, without being told. An endpoint that declares no response
is documented as answering `200`.

## Serving the document

```gleam
import howdy/openapi

let spec =
  openapi.new(title: "Users API", version: "1.0.0")
  |> openapi.description("A small user directory.")
  |> openapi.bearer_auth("bearer")

howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(user.controller())
|> openapi.serve(spec, at: "/openapi.json")
|> openapi.reference(at: "/docs", document: "/openapi.json")
|> howdy.start
```

`openapi.serve` builds the document once, from the controllers mounted
before it, so call it after them. Only routes added as endpoints appear:
ordinary controller routes stay out, so you choose what you publish.

`openapi.reference` serves an interactive API reference, drawn by
[Scalar](https://scalar.com). The page loads Scalar from the jsDelivr CDN,
so a browser viewing it needs to reach `cdn.jsdelivr.net`.

With [Howdy Admin](/docs/admin/api) mounted, the admin finds the document
too, and lets you call each endpoint, signed in as any user.

`openapi.document(spec, app)` gives the document as JSON, to write to a file
for a client generator or check in a test.

Security schemes are declared on the spec with `openapi.bearer_auth`,
`openapi.api_key_header` and `openapi.api_key_cookie`. `openapi.require`
makes one apply to every endpoint. These only document authentication: a
guard or middleware still has to enforce it.

Building a document panics, when the app starts, if an endpoint reads a
path parameter its route does not capture, two different schemas share a
name, or two endpoints share an operation id.

## Versions

An app with a [version group](/docs/middleware/versioning) gets one
document per version. Each holds the app's unversioned endpoints, which
answer whatever the version, and the version's own, including those it falls
back to. Where a version overrides a route, its own endpoint is the one
documented, as it is the one that answers. The document's `info.version` is
the spec's version followed by the group's name in parentheses, such as
`1.0.0 (v2)`, so both the API version and the group it describes are visible.

```gleam
let api =
  version.new(version.path())
  |> version.default("v1")
  |> version.add("v1", [user_v1.controller()])
  |> version.add("v2", [user_v2.controller()])

howdy.new()
|> howdy.versions(api)
|> openapi.serve(spec, at: "/openapi.json")
|> openapi.reference(at: "/docs", document: "/openapi.json")
```

`serve` puts each version's document beside the path it is given:
`/openapi/v1.json` and `/openapi/v2.json`. The path itself serves the
default version's document, or the newest version's when there is no
default. The reference page offers every version, opening on that one.

How a client asks for a version is documented the way OpenAPI expects:

- `version.path()`: paths start with the version, as in `/v2/users`.
- `version.header(name)`: each versioned operation takes the header,
  restricted to that version's name. It is required unless the version is
  the default.
- `version.accept(vendor)`: JSON responses have the vendor media type, such
  as `application/vnd.howdy.v2+json`, so clients send it in `accept`.
- `version.custom(fn)`: the function cannot be described, so paths are
  shown as they are. Say how to choose a version in `openapi.description`.

`openapi.version_document(spec, app, "v2")` gives one version's document as
JSON, and `openapi.versions(app)` lists the versions.

See [`examples/openapi`](https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy/tree/v2.1.1/examples/openapi)
for a complete API.
